\section{Validation Methodology}
\label{sec:direction-validation}

The detailed ten-seed and single-realization reference tables use
the northbound, following-sea records from oceanography-waves-lib v1.4.0.
The 28 ft vessel travels at 5 kn with a $\pm3^\circ$ heading oscillation
of period 20~s. The inputs span four JONSWAP and four PM--Stokes conditions.
Each record lasts 1200~s at 200~Hz. Scoring uses the final 900~s.
For the ten-seed table, paired triplets vary the wave phases, IMU noise
and initialization. The time-series figures use the deterministic sea
direction identified for the individual PDF build.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Stationary direction-validation scenarios.}
\label{tab:direction-scenarios}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
\toprule
Scenario & $H_s$ [m] & $T_p$ [s] & propagation-to azimuth \\
\midrule
W1 & 0.27 & 3.0  & $+30^\circ$ \\
W2 & 1.50 & 5.7  & $-30^\circ$ \\
W3 & 4.00 & 8.5  & $+30^\circ$ \\
W4 & 8.50 & 11.4 & $-30^\circ$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The propagation-plane estimate is axial, so its error is evaluated modulo
$180^\circ$.  For truth axis $\theta$ and estimate $\hat\theta$, define
\begin{equation}
e_{\rm axis}=\frac12\left|\wrap_{(-\pi,\pi]}
\left(2(\hat\theta-\theta)\right)\right|.
\label{eq:axis-error}
\end{equation}
Once travel sense is resolved, the directed propagation-to vector is scored
modulo $360^\circ$.  Travel sense is counted as correct, wrong, or unresolved
against the generator's physical propagation direction.  The implementation's
FORWARD/BACKWARD labels themselves are not treated as physical truth: they are
labels relative to whichever axial representative the estimator returns and
can exchange names when that representative flips by $180^\circ$.
All ten replays are retained. Angular scores are conditional on a finite
estimate; a replay with no resolved travel direction contributes to the
unresolved fraction but has no directed angular score. Summary counts and
paired counts use finite observations and their matched seed intersection.

\subsection{Known-vessel response matching}
The direction branch receives the engine-guarded accelerometer. After levelling,
a direction-only equalizer matches surge, sway and heave amplitude and phase.
In the causal Laplace convention, let
\begin{equation}
Q_j(s)=(1+\tau_j s)^{-2},\qquad
G_h(s)=\left(1+2\zeta_hs/\omega_h+s^2/\omega_h^2\right)^{-1}.
\end{equation}
For the pinned hull, $\tau_{\rm surge}=0.7$ s, $\tau_{\rm sway}=1.0$ s,
$\omega_h=2\pi/2.4$ rad/s and $\zeta_h=0.45$. With
$Q_t=(1+s)^{-2}$, the compensators are $E_j=Q_t/Q_j$ and $E_z=Q_t/G_h$.
They are stable and proper, and their magnitudes do not exceed one.
The common footprint/depth attenuation remains; this is relative response
matching, not incident-wave-height reconstruction. Matching heave as well as
both horizontal channels preserves the orbital quadrature needed for travel
sense. The existing simulation basis conversion exchanges surge and sway at
the input of this stage, so the channel time constants are assigned accordingly.

The wave-period tuner reads the pre-equalized signal. Neither attitude nor
translation receives equalizer output. The profile is explicitly enabled in
the vessel simulators; generic deployment requires a known response in the
correct coordinate frame. Weak response, response nulls and directional spread
can still leave direction unresolved. Confidence and simulation acceptance
thresholds are unchanged. The generator phase kernel
$\cos(\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{x}-\omega t+\phi)$ defines the reported azimuth as
propagation-to, with no additional $180^\circ$ shift.

Table~\ref{tab:direction-rao-ablation} uses the earlier v1.3.1 northbound
records with a 120~s steering period. It compares response matching and
engine guarding on the same eight records for each configuration.

\input{w3d-direction-rao-generated.tex-part}

\paragraph{Response matching in following and head seas}
The v1.4.0 study repeats all three configurations with the engine off
and with 2400~rpm vibration. Table~\ref{tab:direction-both-seas}
gives the fraction of scored samples in each travel-sense class,
averaged over eight records per direction. Each record contributes the
same 900~s window. The 96 replays per direction cover both OU families;
the table shows OU--III.

\begin{table}[t]\centering\footnotesize
\caption{OU--III travel sense, correct / wrong / unresolved (\%).}
\label{tab:direction-both-seas}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}\begin{tabular}{@{}rlll@{}}
\toprule
rpm & Conditioning & Following seas & Head seas \\
\midrule
0 & None & 60.3 / 1.4 / 38.3 & 76.3 / 0.4 / 23.3 \\
0 & Guard & 60.3 / 1.4 / 38.3 & 76.3 / 0.4 / 23.3 \\
0 & Guard + RAO & 63.9 / 0.2 / 35.9 & 70.4 / 0.2 / 29.4 \\
\addlinespace
2400 & None & 1.5 / 4.3 / 94.2 & 31.7 / 3.2 / 65.2 \\
2400 & Guard & 31.3 / 2.7 / 66.1 & 76.5 / 0.3 / 23.2 \\
2400 & Guard + RAO & 61.4 / 0.3 / 38.3 & 70.2 / 0.2 / 29.7 \\
\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{table}

With vibration, adding response matching to the guard raised the correct
fraction from 31.3\% to 61.4\% in following seas. In head seas the
correct fraction fell from 76.5\% to 70.2\%, the unresolved fraction
rose from 23.2\% to 29.7\%, and wrong decisions fell from 0.32\% to
0.17\%. Thus the lower wrong-decision rate in head seas came with more
abstentions. The
\href{https://github.com/bareboat-necessities/ocean-imu/actions/runs/44367501540/artifacts/14463668943}{following-sea}
and
\href{https://github.com/bareboat-necessities/ocean-imu/actions/runs/44367501540/artifacts/14466402720}{head-sea archives}
contain the individual records for both filters.


\section{Results}
\label{sec:direction-results}

Across the eight steady JONSWAP and PM--Stokes sea conditions, angular
statistics use only finite resolved estimates. The mean absolute
circular-mean wave-axis error is
\OUValidationDirectionAbsError{} degrees, the mean of the eight scenario-level
sample-wise axial RMS errors is \OUValidationDirectionRMSE{} degrees, and the worst scenario-level
absolute circular-mean error is \OUValidationDirectionWorstAbsError{} degrees.
Truth-referenced travel sense is correct for \OUValidationTravelCorrect\% of
samples, wrong for \OUValidationTravelWrong\%, and unresolved for
\OUValidationTravelUnresolved\%.  The absolute error of the final directed
estimate is \OUValidationTravelAbsError{} degrees under the aggregate metric
reported by the committed validation bundle.

The southbound ten-seed study repeats all eight scenarios in head seas.
Across 80 OU--III runs in each direction, the mean of the scenario-level
axial RMS errors was $28.04^\circ$ in following seas and $18.75^\circ$
in head seas. Travel sense was correct for 63.3\% and 70.0\% of samples,
respectively. Wrong decisions accounted for 0.2\% in each direction.
The remaining 36.4\% and 29.7\% were unresolved (percentages rounded
independently). Each directional archive retains the finite angular
scores and the sample fractions for each draw.
\href{https://github.com/bareboat-necessities/ocean-imu/actions/runs/44367501540/artifacts/14459213502}{Northbound results} and
\href{https://github.com/bareboat-necessities/ocean-imu/actions/runs/44367501540/artifacts/14465763332}{southbound results}.

These numbers answer two different questions.  The axial statistic measures
whether horizontal vessel motion identifies the incident propagation plane;
the travel-sense statistic measures whether the horizontal--vertical phase test
chooses the correct direction along that plane.  A low axial error therefore
does not, by itself, demonstrate a correct $0$--$360^\circ$ propagation
direction, which is why both stages are scored separately.  Figures
\ref{fig:wave-dir-jonswap-medium}--\ref{fig:wave-dir-pmstokes-medium} show the
same distinction in the generated time-domain diagnostics: direction angle,
uncertainty and confidence are estimator metrics, while the final panel is the
separate travel-sense decision.

\input{w3d-wave-direction-charts.tex-part}

\subsection{Integrated replay with OU--III attitude input}
\label{sec:direction-ou3-integration}

The reported ten-seed direction metrics were obtained from the adaptive OU--III
integrated replay: the direction subsystem receives the levelled attitude and
heading frame from the surrounding marine estimator after startup handoff.
OU--III translational states, its integral pseudo-measurement, and its adaptive
regularization variables are \emph{not} inputs to the direction algorithm.
Table~\ref{tab:direction-ou3-rms} reports the wave-direction RMS errors from
that integrated test, while Table~\ref{tab:direction-ou3-integration} gives the
corresponding single-realization attitude and commitment diagnostics retained in
the original OU--III results section.  This is integration evidence, not an
OU--III performance claim for this paper.

